<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Kamarina</h1><p class="st-meta">598 BCE – 258 BCE (approx.) · capital Kamarina</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Syracusan colony on Sicily's south coast, destroyed and refounded several times, whose restless history became a Greek proverb.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syracuse">Syracuse</a> founded Kamarina around 598 BCE near the mouth of the Hipparis river, on the south-east coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, to extend its reach towards the territory of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gela">Gela</a>. The settlers laid out a regular town on a coastal hill beside a marsh.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Kamarina repeatedly defied its mother city: it revolted around 553 BCE and was destroyed, was refounded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hippocrates-of-gela">Hippocrates of Gela</a> around 492 BCE, emptied again by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelon">Gelon</a> in 484 BCE and refounded from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gela">Gela</a> in 461 BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pindar">Pindar</a> wrote odes for its Olympic victor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psaumis-of-camarina">Psaumis</a>. The Greek proverb 'do not move Camarina', said to come from a disastrous draining of the marsh, warned against stirring up trouble.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Its people evacuated before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthaginians</a> in 405 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timoleon">Timoleon</a> resettled it in the 4th century. In 258 BCE, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-punic-war">First Punic War</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> captured it and sold most inhabitants into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. The site, near Santa Croce Camerina, was excavated from the 19th century.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="city state, 598 BCE – 258 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">city state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Italy</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:47.80%;width:6.77%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>598 BCE – 258 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kamarina</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Doric Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Camarina</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-428&amp;focus=kamarina" aria-label="Kamarina on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5042 849 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kamarina lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5362 1105L5383 1099M5362 1105L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5383 1099h0M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5362 1105h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5362 1105h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kamarina. <a href="/atlas?year=-428&amp;focus=kamarina">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
